            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>1. Define the Audience and Message Before Launch</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Separate countries from languages.</strong> Do not assume English is suitable for Indonesia. Brazilian campaigns should prioritize Brazilian Portuguese, while Spanish-speaking markets require review of regional terminology across Mexico, Colombia, and other countries. Creatives may be tested across regions, but do not assume identical audiences, cultural expectations, or purchasing power.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Use local pricing conventions.</strong> Display prices in local currencies, familiar price units, and relevant promotional formats. Clearly disclose taxes, billing cycles, and refund conditions to prevent purchasing confusion.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Audit account setup.</strong> Verify ad accounts, pages, landing pages, pixels or SDK events, conversion locations, and UTM parameters. Test impressions, clicks, registrations, and purchases separately instead of judging performance by click-through rate alone.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Creative Localization Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Make the copy sound natural.</strong> Review slang, brand names, product names, and calls to action, not just grammar. Local colleagues or a small user sample can provide useful feedback, but linguistic familiarity should not be the only criterion.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Reflect real local scenarios.</strong> Mobile use and messaging apps are common in Southeast Asia, while users in Latin America may prefer to contact sales through WhatsApp. Show locally relevant devices, payment journeys, and product-use situations rather than generic Western visuals.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Screen for cultural and compliance risks.</strong> Check restrictions related to religion, skin tone, alcohol, gambling, minors, politics, and health claims. Avoid prohibited “lowest price” wording, and do not include unverified performance promises.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Align landing-page details.</strong> The ad headline, button, offer, and landing page must communicate the same promise. A “free trial” creative that leads directly to a payment page may generate healthy clicks but weak conversion rates.</li>
</ul>
<h2>3. Budget and Risk Controls</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Test with segmented budgets.</strong> Break budgets down by country, language, and creative. Do not immediately concentrate spend in one country because early acquisition costs appear low. Allow enough time for the complete conversion cycle before comparing customer acquisition cost and payment success rates.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Set frequency and stop-loss rules.</strong> Adjust creatives or pause campaigns when predefined frequency, cost-per-click, or invalid-signup thresholds are reached. For high-ticket products, do not make budget decisions based on click-through rate alone.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Confirm payment and funding workflows.</strong> Check supported currencies, payment methods, account balances, credit limits, and billing entities. Set balance alerts and manual review procedures for insufficient funds, failed payments, or shared balances across multiple ad accounts.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Weekly Reconciliation Routine</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Maintain a market-level media ledger.</strong> Record ad accounts, currencies, top-up amounts, platform spend, taxes or FX differences, payment outcomes, and billing periods. Do not mistake the remaining account balance for actual media spend.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Trace the full conversion path.</strong> Compare platform reports with sales and payment data, focusing on duplicate events, attribution-window differences, agency fees, failed payments, and refunds. Flag anomalies by country, channel, and creative.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Keep auditable versions.</strong> Log every change to audiences, language, bids, creatives, and landing pages, with dates and version identifiers. This makes it possible to determine whether growth resulted from localization improvements or from budget, seasonality, or promotional changes.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Core principle: Verify the consistency of “audience–creative promise–landing page–payment method–conversion tracking” before scaling spend.</p>
</blockquote>
